                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Sausage Sports Club[/h1]\n\nThe wackiest and zaniest of party games featuring a colorful cast of weenie-shaped animals and a whole lot of pretty insane mini-games to dash and flop about in. Explore each mini-game through a village of friendly animal characters, each with their own little problems and rivalries that come at you in the form of small quests.\n\nIt's an unhinged and fast-paced sort of high-octane party game with a lot going on, oh and when it comes to collectable hats [i]Sausage Sports Club[/i] knows no rival.\n\n[h1]Gameplay[/h1]\n\nAs silly as that sounds (and trust me, silly it is) [i]Sausage Sports Club[/i] is made even more ridiculous and fun thanks to its crazy physics and hilarious wobbling movements of our rotund animal protagonists. Awkwardly jump, wobble and dash your way to each game mode's objective and smack your way to a sausage sports victory.\n\nThe different fun-filled competitive mini-games presented here have you utilizing your noodle-like body shape to its full potential, out-wriggling and out-smacking the other players on the field. A good deal of these mini-games involve each animal player smacking their weenie-shaped body wildly into an array of flying balls to hit some sort of goal, like some crazy kind of barnyard soccer match. That's right, weenies and balls.\n\nHuge battles of animal soccer, weenie-body smacking Sumo action, or intense hands-on capture-the-flag matches with slipper tackles. There's even a Paintball mode with a bit of a [i]Splatoon[/i]-style twist of competing to see which team can cover the map in their own vibrant color. I really wish there were even more party games here, because what we have is insanely hectic and fun. Luckily, the adventure mode campaign doles them out nice and slow, with a fun and engaging quest-based progression.\n\n[i]Sausage Sports Club[/i] is nothing short of chaotic. It's not a simple or particularly refined sort of party game, and that's okay because when it comes to party games crazy is good. It just goes all out, usually with 10 or more animal contestants making a mad dash for the same target, flopping against each other with full phallic force. The CPUs don't mess around either, and will waste no time punishing a learning player.\n\n[h1]Final Thought[/h1]\n\nObviously, [i]Sausage Sports Club[/i] is at its best with a few good friends around to take part in the laugh-inducing hijinx and compete for bragging rights. Though thanks to a fully scripted progressive Story mode with quests and some entertainingly reckless CPUs to fumble around with, a full-fledged campaign is here to tackle solo or with your usual couch co-op buddies.\n\n[i]This review made possible through the generosity, consideration and contribution of [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/11739878-ReviewExperts/]Review Experts(REXnetwork)[/url] and the developer/publisher. \n\nEmail contact@dnbmedia.co for requests & promotions[/i]",
